# .gitattributes — merge behaviour for paths whose bytes belong to a GENERATOR, not an author.
#
# These two roots are committed generator output: the SDK has to exist before the app compiles,
# and the wire bindings before either backend does. Every merge conflict they produce is resolved
# the same way — throw both sides away and regenerate — so stopping the merge to ask a human is
# pure ceremony.
#
# The driver keeps `ours` and exits 0. It does NOT regenerate: `bun sdk` / `bun contracts` does
# that, by REGENERATING AND COMPARING, and it is already a step of the battery. The authority over
# generated bytes is the generator invoked by a gate that can afford to take its time, never a
# conflict resolution running inside git's index window.
#
# BOTH HALVES ARE REQUIRED. This file says WHICH paths use the driver; it cannot say what the
# driver IS (git refuses to take an executable from a tracked file, for obvious reasons). Each
# clone declares that once:
#
#   git config merge.regenerate.driver "bun scripts/sync/merge-regenerate.ts %O %A %B %P"
#
# Without that line git falls back to the default driver and you get ordinary conflicts — noisy,
# but never wrong. scripts/sync/merge-regenerate.ts is the source of truth for both strings.
#
# Ported manually from template-fullstack (scripts/sync/** sync train, 2026-08-13) — this file
# itself is NOT part of the declared `inherited` surface in sync.yaml (it lives at repo root, not
# under scripts/sync/** or scripts/lib/**), but merge-regenerate.ts/test.ts arrived as part of
# that surface and are non-functional without it. Candidate to fold into `inherited` explicitly
# in a future train.

packages/client/dist/**            merge=regenerate
packages/contracts/generated/**    merge=regenerate
